# Error Handling Rules

## Server Action Return Structure

Server Actions should return structured results instead of throwing exceptions.

```ts
// Standard return type
type ActionResult<T = void> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: string }

// Example action
export async function createThread(input: unknown): Promise<ActionResult<{ slug: string }>> {
  try {
    const parsed = schema.safeParse(input)
    if (!parsed.success) return { success: false, error: "Invalid data" }

    const thread = await db.thread.create({ ... })
    return { success: true, data: { slug: thread.slug } }
  } catch (e) {
    console.error("[createThread]", e)
    return { success: false, error: "Something went wrong, please try again" }
  }
}
```

## Error Messages

- **Do not expose internal error details** to users (DB errors, stack traces, etc.)
- Messages shown to users must be clear and user-friendly
- Developer logs in the format: `console.error("[actionName]", error)`

## Next.js Error Boundaries

- Define `error.tsx` for every route segment
- Global error: `src/app/error.tsx`
- Not found: `src/app/not-found.tsx`
- `error.tsx` must have `"use client"`

```tsx
// src/app/error.tsx
"use client"
export default function GlobalError({ reset }: { reset: () => void }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong</h2>
      <button onClick={reset}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## notFound and redirect

- Use `notFound()` when a resource is not found — shows the 404 page
- Use `redirect("/login")` when unauthorized — do not throw exceptions for 401/403
- Do not wrap `notFound()` and `redirect()` in try/catch — Next.js throws them as special exceptions

```ts
// Correct
const thread = await db.thread.findUnique({ where: { slug } })
if (!thread) notFound()

// Wrong
try {
  const thread = await db.thread.findUniqueOrThrow(...)
} catch {
  notFound() // try/catch swallows redirect/notFound
}
```

## Form Errors

- Catch Server Action errors with `useActionState`
- Set up a structure capable of showing a separate error message per field
- Use toast only for general success/error notifications; use inline messages for field errors

## Logging

- Do not leave `console.log` in production — use only `console.error` and `console.warn`
- Route logs through `src/lib/logger.ts` for future Sentry/Betterstack integration
- Do not log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, personal information)
